# Marketing platform: the proxy site (plan M7 decisions 1 and 2, Task 7). The server's proxy is Caddy; this is the
# one site file it gets, and nothing else in its configuration changes except the global block below.
#
# A TEMPLATE. Render it with the placeholders replaced (docs/operations.md, "Proxy site"), never by hand-editing:
#   __MKT_HOST__       the site address: marketing.sarathi.gvcc.in, or the test hostname until A2.
#   __MKT_TLS__        one line, as the host issues certificates: `tls /certs/<name>.pem /certs/<name>.key`
#                      (a Cloudflare origin certificate), `import stg <name>` (the staging snippet), or nothing
#                      (an empty line) for Caddy's automatic HTTPS.
#   __MKT_CORE__       the core:      127.0.0.1:4100 (Caddy on the host) or mkt-core:4000 (Caddy in Docker,
#                                     with infra/prod/compose.proxy-network.yaml).
#   __MKT_DASHBOARD__  the dashboard: 127.0.0.1:3100, or mkt-dashboard:3000.
#
# The client's address. Caddy works out {client_ip} from the Caddyfile's GLOBAL options, which a site file
# cannot set. They must say (every range in infra/prod/proxy/cloudflare-ips.txt, dated):
#   {
#     servers {
#       trusted_proxies static <every range in cloudflare-ips.txt>
#       client_ip_headers CF-Connecting-IP
#     }
#   }
# From a Cloudflare address {client_ip} is CF-Connecting-IP; from anywhere else it is the connection's own
# address, whatever headers came with it. X-Forwarded-For is then REPLACED with that one address, never
# appended to: the core trusts one proxy (TRUST_PROXY=1, the invite limiter reads the last entry) and Better
# Auth trusts X-Forwarded-For only when it holds one address (the sign-in limiter). A forged CF-Connecting-IP or
# X-Forwarded-For sent around Cloudflare therefore changes nothing (Review Focus 1; smoke check forged-*).
#
# What passes unchanged (docs/progress.md, proxy preconditions of milestones 2b, 2c, 4 and 6): the path and
# query string as received (handle, not handle_path: nothing is stripped or rewritten), the Host header,
# Authorization, the X-Mkt-* trigger headers, the raw body (no buffering, no decoding) and DELETE bodies.
__MKT_HOST__ {
	__MKT_TLS__

	# Body sizes. Everything is capped at 1 MiB (the core's own JSON limit is 1 MB) except one path: the document
	# upload, POST /api/v1/brands/<brand uuid>/onboarding/documents (exactly that path, no trailing segment), which takes a PDF
	# of up to 100 MB (the core caps it itself by type) and so gets 101 MiB. The worker's checkpoints (up to 8 MB)
	# never pass this proxy: in production the worker reaches the core directly (CORE_URL http://core:4000).
	# Named matchers, so exactly one request_body applies to each request.
	@documentUpload {
		method POST
		path_regexp ^/api/v1/brands/[0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}/onboarding/documents$
	}
	@notDocumentUpload not {
		method POST
		path_regexp ^/api/v1/brands/[0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}/onboarding/documents$
	}
	request_body @documentUpload {
		max_size 101MiB
	}
	request_body @notDocumentUpload {
		max_size 1MiB
	}

	# HSTS and COOP belong to the proxy (apps/dashboard/next.config.ts); the app sets the rest. Deferred, so they
	# replace the core's own copies (helmet) instead of being sent twice.
	header {
		defer
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
		Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy "same-origin"
		-Server
	}

	encode zstd gzip

	# /api/ to the core; everything else, /api without the slash included, to the dashboard.
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy __MKT_CORE__ {
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
		}
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy __MKT_DASHBOARD__ {
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
		}
	}
}
